              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say माम्, “me”. Then recall the Sanskrit number reserved for exactly two: the dual. Now make the object be the speaker and one other person."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: आवाम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "आवाम् (āvām) means “us two” as the object."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सः आवाम् पश्यति। saḥ āvām paśyati. He sees us two."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Whitney §491 lists आवाम् (āvām) as the independent dual form used for both subject and object. Here its position in the known frame makes the object job clear. Hold the first vowel long: ā-vām."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "Taken Apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "As a grammatical form, आवाम् (āvām) comes from the first-person pronoun paradigm; it is not a word from a separate lexical root. Whitney's Sanskrit Grammar §491 records it as the independent dual used for both subject and object. This lesson makes no deeper historical derivation claim."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Source: Whitney, Sanskrit Grammar (1889), §491."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
